@meta
id: 21-22
title: Scenario — the fixture migration
chapter: 21
type: scenario
skills: macro-recursive, macro-record, macro-count, macro-play
par: 250
minutes: 18
vscode: four files, four different multi-cursor sessions, and a regex you will get wrong twice
open: orders.fixture.ts customers.fixture.ts order-status.ts seed.spec.ts seed.ts

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-540  Seed data moves from CSV exports to typed fixtures.
             Convert the two exports, give the status enum string
             values, and make the seed spec async.

Five files are open, in this order. `:bn` between them, `:wqa` at
the end. Four of them change; each wants its own macro.

  1. orders.fixture.ts — fourteen rows of `id,customerId,total,currency`
     become records:

         { id: 'ord_101', customerId: 'cus_7', total: 1200, currency: 'GBP' },

  2. customers.fixture.ts — eight rows of `id|name|email` become
     records:

         { id: 'cus_7', name: 'Grace Lin', email: 'grace.lin@example.com' },

  3. order-status.ts — every enum member gets a string value equal to
     its name, as in lesson 1. Eight members.

  4. seed.spec.ts — three tests need `async` on the `it(` line and
     `await` after the `=` on the line below.

  5. seed.ts — imports all of the above. Nothing to change.

Use a different register for each macro. If one goes wrong you want
to be able to fix that one and leave the others alone.

@keys
| `qaq` then `qa` … `@a` `q` | a recursive macro with a guard |
| `qb` … `q`, `7@b` | a counted macro |
| `qc` … `q`, `99@c` | a search-driven macro |
| `:reg a b c d` | see all four at once |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer; write all and quit |

@hints
Both fixtures end in `];`. A guard that finds the separator, `^f,`
in one and `^f|` in the other, fails on that line, so both macros can
be recursive and neither needs a count.
--
The enum is lesson 1 with `$x` first because the members end in a
comma. The spec is lesson 7: search inside the macro, `j0` before the
`f=`, and `99@d` until the search fails.
--
Orders: `qaq`, `qa^f,s', customerId: '<Esc>f,s', total: <Esc>f,s, currency: '<Esc>^i{ id: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>j@aq`, `@a`.
Customers: the same shape with `f|` and `name`, `email`.
--
If a macro misbehaves on one line, `u` until the file is clean,
`:reg` to read what you recorded, and either re-record into the same
register or paste, fix and `0"ay$`.

@solution
One route, buffer by buffer.

**orders.fixture.ts** — `j`, `qaq`, then
`qa^f,s', customerId: '<Esc>f,s', total: <Esc>f,s, currency: '<Esc>^i{ id: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>j@aq`,
then `@a`. Guard first, `@a` last, one press.

**customers.fixture.ts** — `:bn`, `j`, `qbq`, then
`qb^f|s', name: '<Esc>f|s', email: '<Esc>^i{ id: '<Esc>A' },<Esc>j@bq`,
then `@b`.

**order-status.ts** — `:bn`, `j`, `qc$x^yiwA = '<Esc>pa',<Esc>jq`,
then `7@c`.

**seed.spec.ts** — `:bn`, `qd/it(.*, () =><Enter>f(;iasync <Esc>j0f=wiawait <Esc>q`,
then `99@d`.

**seed.ts** — nothing. `:wqa`.

Around two hundred and twenty keystrokes for thirty-three lines across
four files, and not one of them was typed twice.

This is the chapter in one sitting. Four macros, three shapes:
recursive with a guard for a block of data that ends in something the
guard rejects, counted for a short block where the count is on the
screen, and search-driven for targets that are scattered. Each was
recorded once, on one line, while looking at it.

The thing to take away is not any one macro. It is that when the
second identical line appears, the decision is already made. You
record. If it is right, you play it until it fails. If it is wrong,
it is text in a register and you fix it. That reflex is what the
chapter was for; the tools around it, `:normal`, `:g`, `:cdo`,
`:argdo`, only decide how far one recording travels.

